# Network Incident Intelligence Platform

Automated root cause analysis and outage reconstruction for multi-device network environments.

---

## Why I built this

When a network goes down, the typical response is manual: SSH into each device, grep through syslog, cross-reference timestamps, and try to figure out what broke first. On a multi-device network that process takes 15–30 minutes — and every minute the wrong thing gets blamed, or the right thing gets fixed in the wrong order, costs more downtime.

I wanted to build a system that does that correlation automatically. Give it a network topology, let it collect telemetry continuously, and when something breaks it should tell you exactly what failed, what cascaded from it, and what to do about it — in under 60 seconds.

---

## How it works

Three Python scripts run continuously and work together:

**`syslog_collector.py`** listens on UDP 514 on the GNS3 VM. Every syslog message from every network device arrives here, gets parsed for severity and device identity, and gets written to PostgreSQL on the Windows host.

**`snmp_poller.py`** polls devices via SNMP every 30 seconds, checking reachability. Results go into the same PostgreSQL database.

**`incident_engine.py`** runs on Windows and queries the database every 30 seconds. It looks at the last 10 minutes of events, applies pattern-matching rules against known failure signatures, walks the topology graph to find downstream blast radius, and writes a structured incident record — including root cause device, affected devices, timeline, and remediation steps.

The Grafana dashboard pulls from PostgreSQL directly and auto-refreshes every 30 seconds. When an incident fires you see it immediately: the active incident counter turns red, the incident log shows the full root cause analysis, and the syslog stream shows the raw events that triggered it.

---

## Architecture

```
9 GNS3 network devices
        (Cisco IOU)
         |                   |
   syslog UDP 514        SNMP every 30s
         |                   |
syslog_collector.py    snmp_poller.py
    (GNS3 VM)             (GNS3 VM)
         |                   |
         +--------+----------+
                  |
            PostgreSQL
          (Windows host)
           3 tables:
     syslog_events
     snmp_metrics
     incidents
                  |
        incident_engine.py
          (Windows host)
                  |
       +----------+-----------+
       |                      |
  Grafana dashboard    Incident reports
  (real-time panels)   (root cause + fix)
```

---

## Network topology

9 devices across 4 tiers running OSPF:

```
                    Edge-RTR-01
                   /           \
            Core-SW-01       Core-SW-02
                |                 |
            Dist-HQ          Dist-Branch
           /       \               |
       Acc-SW1   Acc-SW2        Acc-SW3
```

The correlation engine knows this topology. When Core-SW-01 loses an interface, the engine walks the tree and identifies that Dist-HQ, Acc-SW1, Acc-SW2, and everything below them are downstream — those are symptoms, not causes.

---

## What the engine detects

| Event type | Detection logic |
|---|---|
| Interface down | `LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN` + `down` in syslog |
| OSPF neighbor lost | `OSPF` + `ADJCHG` + `DOWN` in syslog |
| Device unreachable | SNMP reports `unreachable` for 90+ seconds |
| Link flapping | 5+ up/down events from same device in 10 minutes |

Resolution is detected the same way in reverse. When the interface comes back up or OSPF adjacency restores, the incident is marked resolved and the dashboard turns green.

There's one non-obvious fix worth calling out: after an incident resolves, the 10-minute event window still contains the original failure syslog messages. Without handling this, the engine would immediately re-detect the same incident. The fix is a `recently_resolved` dict that tracks `(device, incident_type) → resolved_at` timestamp. On each engine cycle, any matching event older than that timestamp gets skipped.

---

## Sample incident output

```
============================================================
  INCIDENT DETECTED -- 2026-06-01 02:57:28
============================================================
  Type:        OSPF_NEIGHBOR_LOST
  Root Cause:  Core-SW-01
  Event:       OSPF adjacency lost at 2026-06-01 02:57:22
  Affected:    Dist-HQ, Acc-SW1, Acc-SW2
  Remediation: Verify OSPF config on both sides.
               Run: show ip ospf neighbor
               Check interface status.
------------------------------------------------------------
  Timeline:
    02:57:22 | Core-SW-01 | %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Nbr 1.1.1.1 from FULL to DOWN
    02:57:24 | Core-SW-01 | %LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: Ethernet0/0 changed to down
============================================================
```

---

## Tech stack

| Component | Tool |
|---|---|
| Network simulator | GNS3 |
| Network devices | Cisco IOU-L3, Cisco IOU-L2 |
| Telemetry | Python syslog server (UDP 514) + subprocess snmpget |
| Database | PostgreSQL 16 |
| Dashboards | Grafana |
| Language | Python 3 |

---

## Running it

**Prerequisites:** GNS3 with Cisco IOU images loaded, Python 3.9+, PostgreSQL, Grafana.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ascjreddy/Network-Monitoring-Incident-Response-Platform
cd Network-Monitoring-Incident-Response-Platform
pip install psycopg2-binary
```

Set up the database (create the three tables — schema in `architecture.md`).

Then start the three scripts:

```bash
# On GNS3 VM — terminal 1
sudo python3 syslog_collector.py

# On GNS3 VM — terminal 2
sudo python3 snmp_poller.py

# On Windows — PowerShell
python incident_engine.py
```

Import the Grafana dashboard from `grafana-dashboard.json`.

To trigger a test failure on Core-SW-01:

```
conf t
interface Ethernet0/0
shutdown
end
```

The engine detects it within 60 seconds. Bring it back with `no shutdown` and the incident resolves automatically.

---

## Test results

**Test Case 1 — Core-SW-01 interface failure**

Triggered by shutting Ethernet0/0 on Core-SW-01. The engine detected OSPF_NEIGHBOR_LOST and traced the cascade to Dist-HQ and downstream access switches. Incident was resolved automatically when the interface came back up.

Detection time: under 60 seconds from interface shutdown to incident record written.

Additional test scenarios covering distribution-tier failure, OSPF adjacency loss, and false-positive prevention are documented in the screenshots folder.

